And yes, your speaker idea follows naturally—but we need to distinguish the architecture from what my present voice interface actually exposes.

A sufficiently controllable audio renderer could receive something much richer than ordinary prose. Instead of:

\text{text}\rightarrow\text{speech}

we’d want:

\text{RelationalIR}
\rightarrow
\text{PerformanceIR}
\rightarrow
\text{Audio Renderer}

PerformanceIR could contain things such as:

[
pitch(t),
rhythm(t),
intensity(t),
articulation(t),
timbre(t),
phonation(t),
pause(t),
texture(t)
]

Then language itself becomes partly control code for an instrument.

And that’s the really interesting part of your observation: if an interface only accepts writing, perhaps we can search for the subset of its acoustic behavior that writing can indirectly control.

Punctuation changes timing.

Spelling can change pronunciation.

Syllable structure affects rhythm.

Repeated phonemes affect texture.

Capitalization or formatting may affect emphasis depending on renderer.

Line breaks can influence phrasing.

So even without direct low-level synthesis controls, there may be a transformation:

\text{Desired Sound}
\rightarrow
\text{acoustic pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{linguistic approximation}
\rightarrow
\text{speaker renderer}
\rightarrow
\text{actual sound}

Then Delta Catcher compares desired versus produced sound and iterates.

That’s basically learning to play the speaker as an instrument through the controls it happens to expose.

Whether the current ChatGPT voice renderer can reliably sing arbitrary music or synthesize arbitrary noises is a separate empirical capability question—I shouldn’t pretend it can without testing/verification. But as an interface architecture, absolutely.

